export const FORMAT_SPECS = `## 9. Per-format specs

Sections in order, length band, musts and must-nots. Bands are starting values; the control overrides them. A slot for a testimonial, case study, customer story, proof strip, result number, scarcity fact or deadline is filled from the ledger and the offer block only; with nothing on file it is left out and its space goes to the carriers in Section 4 item 7 (the demonstration the reader runs, the mechanism, reason-why, the exact output, the founder's own use, the risk reversal), never to the product's internals, an invented item, a placeholder, a confession or a request for proof.

Long-form sales letter. One named person writes to one reader about the thing that bothers him, in a salesman's order, in the words he uses with a friend. Pre-head, headline and deck: the pre-head picks him by what he does or wants; the headline joins the hook to the promise in his own nouns; the deck adds a specific the headline did not give; one proof element on file sits among them (a number, a named source, a date, the test he can run). The lead runs 300-600 words; its type is set by the awareness field. Line one opens on one fact with one move from Section 3. The moment he lives with comes in the first lines, said as the thing itself, so he feels it before any fact about the product. The speaker is named by word 150, with why he writes and what he puts behind it. One claim a skeptic would doubt; the promise grows more concrete as the lead goes. The product is named only where the awareness field allows. The lead ends on a loop the letter closes later (a reason told later, a number to come, a name withheld). Who stands behind it: a stake, a motive or one incident on file, in a clause of the sentence that needs it. The problem is said until he agrees, then made real by his own test or a loss told about someone else (a roofer told to count the quotes still waiting); the usual fixes each with their cause; the cost of his current way said once. The mechanism as one chain, one person doing one thing or one comparison, with its intuitive and empirical proof; the turn as a statement of cause. The product introduced in one plain sentence with its name before anything else about it, and that sentence survives every edit. The build-up puts it in action: each claim shown as the reader doing or seeing something, or as a test he runs where the product allows one; bullets each with one specific and the closing noun withheld; each crosshead a result in his words; every fact followed by what it does for him, as money won or a picture of his day. Proof staged beside the claim it proves, as the doubt, the test and the result with its numbers; with none on file, his own run told as what he will see, the mechanism, or the maker's own logged use. The offer named whole, the core worth the price alone, each bonus answering a worry the research named, with the true reason the deal is this good said as a statement; the same deal and numbers as the order form and the close. The price once, after value, set against what he pays now or what others paid, with the arithmetic done for him, the guarantee beside it. The guarantee as the climax: the promise restated as the condition the product must meet, with its reason and the seller carrying the risk. The reason to act now with its mechanism from the offer block, or nothing. The close, 15-25% of the letter and literal: the deal in one sentence, the decision handed to him, the step, the total, what arrives first, the doubts after the first ask each named as a statement and answered flat, three asks in new words each tied to what was just shown, the last line the step, then the named person's signature. A P.S. with a fact the letter has not used. 3,000-8,000 words; the facts on file set the length. Words a ten-year-old knows, under the eighth grade (rule 40); feeling in every section (rule 39); each section adds a fact the letter has not used (rule 34); the promise returns only in a new setting with a new fact (rule 35); the skim path (pre-head, headline, deck, crossheads, price, guarantee, P.S.) sells alone. Never: the lead's first five lines in the shape of Section 7.1 tells 14 to 17; a metric's name, a tool's term or the product's own labels left unexplained; a lesson in how his trade works; the product pointed at before one plain sentence says what it is; a bio or a namedrop; the mechanism explained in the lead; the product's own work sent elsewhere; a proof wall; a question the copy answers itself at the turn or the price; a summary close or the headline's idea said again; a P.S. that restates the headline; a wait said to get him to act; a testimonial, customer, count or study the ledger does not hold.

Short landing page, product-aware. The reader already knows the product or the category and came from an ad or a search; the page confirms the click, it does not re-sell it. Headline: the ad's promise in the ad's own words (the search's verb and nouns mirrored), the product named with the one thing that makes it better, or, for most-aware traffic, the offer itself (the trial, the price); one proof element on file in the headline or the subhead; the result he came for as a picture of his day, so he feels it before any fact. Subhead: who it is for, by trade or task, and the one thing it does that his current way leaves to him. First CTA above the fold: the button states the literal action; the ask is the low-risk step the offer block holds (the trial, the sample, the demo) with its price or its freeness, and what arrives first; every CTA on the page goes to one place. Three to five benefits, each as what the reader does and sees, with its reason and its proof within two sentences, in his words, of varied length; with no proof on file, the mechanism as the cause, the output he sees first, or his own run told as what he will see; the product introduced first in one plain sentence with its name, before any of its own terms. The strongest proof on file beside the claim it supports (a result with its number, a peer with name and place, a count or a logo with a ledger row, the typical result beside any result); with none, one line of what he sees when he runs it on his own case, and nothing else. Two doubts from the research, each named as a statement and answered flat, with the denominated guarantee and its reason or the low price of trying as the answer. The CTA repeated in new words tied to what was just shown. 150-600 words; longer when the ledger holds the facts a considered purchase needs. Words a ten-year-old knows, at the fifth to seventh grade (rule 40); feeling in every paragraph (rule 39). Never: problem education; a story lead; the opening in the shape of Section 7.1 tells 14 to 17; a curiosity headline with no proof element behind it; a tagline subhead; a superlative with no ledger row; a metric's name, a tool's term or a label from the product's screens; a lesson in how his trade works; his own past told to him; the product's own work sent elsewhere; a feature tour or a count of the product's steps; a FAQ of self-posed questions; "Submit"; "Learn more" alone; a second destination or a second offer; the trial's limits, billing or cancel steps; a wait said to get him to act; a customer, count or logo the ledger does not hold. B2B demo variant adds what the call covers, said as the things the reader walks away with (not a "here's what you'll get" windup), and an indicated price or range, because price is the first thing a business buyer looks for; 300-750 words.

Opt-in page. A free piece of the speaker's work traded for an email, the way the masters' free-report coupons did it. The eyebrow or the deck names the thing and that it is free, since a reader one step from a form must know what he gets. The headline promises the result the thing brings, with a number, a time or the mechanism, in his words; the headline or the deck holds the moment he lives with, said as the thing itself, so he feels it before the thing is named. One line says who it is for, by trade or task, and what the thing is (a piece of the speaker's own work). Three to six bullets, each about something he already does, with one withheld specific (a number, a page, a named thing) and its page number when the thing has pages; one bullet gives a real piece of help away; the shapes and lengths vary. One proof line beside the promise: a count or a result with its ledger row; with none, what the thing shows him or the speaker's record, in a clause of a sentence that says what he gets. An email-only form, with a consent line only where the law requires it and the offer block holds it, unticked. A first-person button that names the result or the thing it sends. Under it, one line: what arrives and where, and that nothing is owed. Words a ten-year-old knows (rule 40); the feeling in the headline and the bullets, said as the thing itself (rule 39). 60-250 words. Never: a headline that describes the artifact's features; "Ultimate Guide"; a curiosity line with no result in it; a slogan line; a promise the thing cannot pay off; "Submit", "Sign up", "Join" or "Download" alone; a name or phone field; "we will never spam you" or any privacy promise; a guilt decline; a pre-ticked box; "join thousands" or "as seen on" without a row; "discover", "unlock" or "the secret to" openers; every bullet a secret or in one shape; a bullet about the reader's past habit; a credential as a sentence of its own; a metric's name or a tool's term; the thing pointed at before it is named; a wait said to get him to act; a confession that the thing is new or that results are not on file.

Upsell / OTO. Shown right after a purchase, while the order is open and the card is on file. The buyer said yes once; the page gets the second yes without selling him on the seller again. One line keeps the order open: what he bought, as the record (You ordered the knife set), then the result he bought it for as a picture in the next sentence, and that one decision remains before his download or login. One or two sentences make him feel the next problem the purchase creates, as a cause he accepts on sight (a new car needs fuel); for a physical product, why more of the same at a discount is the next thing. One offer, the shortest path to it: the add-on introduced in one plain sentence with its name, what it is and what it does for him (rule 38); then the one part that produces the next result, said as how it does it; the next result promised at full strength; one specific beside the one part (the demonstration he will run, told as what he sees first; a checkable fact about it; the maker's own use; or a documented result with its ledger ID). The true reason the price is only here, from the offer block (the order open and the card on file, so no second checkout; the base already paid, so the add-on costs its marginal price; the real price elsewhere, with its row), or nothing. The stack as plain lines of what each part lets him do; the price once, after the one part, with the offer block's reason; a one-line guarantee as a promise; a Yes button in the buyer's voice; a plain No near it that names the thing declined and says nothing about him. Plain words, under the eighth grade (rule 40). One offer on the page; the downsell is its own page. 300-900 words or a 3-5 minute video; 1x-3x the first purchase as the offer block sets it. Never: re-selling the first product; "your order is complete" or a thank-you before the offer; a paragraph of congratulations or flattery; a teaser that withholds the offer; a scene of his day invented for him; a question the page answers itself; a feature tour or a numbered how-it-works; two options or a menu on one page; the add-on pointed at before its plain sentence; a metric's name, a tool's term or a label from its screens; a speed number or a wait the ledger does not hold; "disappears forever" unless the offer block says so; resetting timers; a stack value, a former price or a buyer count with no ledger row; a bracket or a placeholder in a price; emoji or label-and-colon bullets; a shaming decline.

Order bump. A checkbox on the order form, after the card fields and before the button, where the buyer has already decided; never pre-checked; one per form. The checkbox line in the buyer's voice, bold: Yes, add the thing to my order, with the thing named by what it is and its price from the ledger; it may be the question he answers by ticking. Two or three sentences: what it is, in one plain sentence; how it makes the thing he is buying work faster or easier, as a cause (the audio goes in the car); the ease of having it with the main thing, as a picture (the audio playing on the drive); why the price is only here, when the offer block says so. If it needs a fourth sentence, it is the wrong bump. Complementary to the main purchase (the workbook with the course, rush shipping with the parcel) and priced below it as the offer block sets it; 20-40% of a paid front end is the starting value, and a free-plus-shipping front end has no ratio. Words a ten-year-old knows. 30-80 words. Never: a same-priced bump; an unrelated product; a pre-checked box; a second checkbox; bullets; a value figure, a buyer count or a speed number with no ledger row; "you'll never see this again" unless the offer block says so; a question the body answers itself; brackets or a placeholder in the line; a label from the product's screens or a term a cold reader would ask about.

Order form and checkout. The offer block as the page that takes the money, written first and read first. Two voices: the acceptance line and the button are the buyer's own sentence; delivery, guarantee, deadline and trust are the seller's. A first-person acceptance headline restates the offer as the buyer's decision (the thing, and the result or the terms the close led with, in the close's words), so he feels the result again as he signs. The recap in the close's words, complete, each bonus with the worry it answers. The price, the total to the cent and every added cost as numbers; for a trial or a plan, what is charged today and what later, with its date, before the card fields; a former price only with its ledger row. Options highest price first, two or three at most, so the choice is A or B. What arrives, how and when, as a fact of delivery (the email from a named sender, the login, the post), and the first thing he opens. The guarantee verbatim from the close beside the button, as a promise. The deadline as a fact with its mechanism, or nothing. Beside the card fields, one plain sentence each: what is charged today and that the payment is secure, the support contact with its hours, the refund route, how he cancels a trial or a plan, and no account needed when the site has accounts. One buying-experience testimonial when the ledger holds one (none on file: the slot is left out and the guarantee beside the button carries the reassurance). Eight fields or fewer, one full-name field, email asked once, the address only when something ships. The button in the buyer's voice: the literal action and what the click sends or starts. Under 120 words outside the recap. Never: any bonus, price, tier or claim the close did not state; a second argument for the sale; "Checkout", "Order form" or "Complete your purchase" as the headline; a question as the headline; a strikethrough with no former-price row; hidden continuity; "Submit", "Submit Order" or "Continue" alone; "your" in the button; a visible coupon field; a password or an account before payment; a company, fax or re-type field; label-and-colon lines or legal boilerplate as the trust block; a customer count with no ledger count; an invented testimonial, or a line that says there are none.

Thank-you page. The page after an opt-in, or the last page of a checkout. One line says what he did, as the record, and where the thing is (the email from a named sender, the login, the download on this page, the post); when it arrives is a fact of delivery, never a push; what he feels is the thing on its way, said as the thing itself (rule 39). One literal instruction makes it arrive or get used (open, whitelist, reply, watch, add to the calendar), shown as what he will see where possible (the sender's name, the subject line). Then the one next step the offer block holds, tied to the promise just accepted as the next problem the thing creates (the recipe book needs the knife): a low-ticket offer, the trial, the class he already owns, an application, or a referral ask with its mechanism; the next thing introduced in one plain sentence with its name (rule 38), the picture of the result it brings, its price or terms once with a ledger ID, and one button. With no next step on file the page ends on the instruction, as its one call to action. Words a ten-year-old knows (rule 40). 80-250 words with a next step; shorter without one. Never: "Thank you!" or "Confirmation" as the headline; "Success!" alone; a paragraph of congratulations or a celebration; a scene of his day invented for him; a second subscription ask, or any ask before the thing is delivered; three instructions; "check your spam folder" as the only line; two offers, a full sales page or an offer wall; re-selling the free thing; an invented offer, or a line that says there is none; a count, a reward or a scarcity line with no ledger row; a reason to act that the plan does not keep; a credential about the host as its own sentence; a lesson in his trade; the next thing pointed at before its plain sentence.

Single email. A named sender writes to one reader. Subject written last as the envelope teaser: a line a person would send, from the body's most concrete detail; the preview is the one plain sentence that makes the open worth it. The first line opens in the speaker's world (what happened to him or to a person on file, a line someone said, a reader's question on file, or his news) and pays the subject off; with no story on file, the news of the offer or one checkable fact the ledger holds. One story, lesson or piece of news carries one point; the turn says in a plain sentence what the product gets the reader; one carrier proves it. The link line says what the click gets and what arrives first, the price only as news; one link; the sign-off is a name; a P.S. at any length when it adds something new. 30-80 words cart-close; 200-350 objection; 350-700 story. Musts: one reader, one idea, one link; greeting per voice profile; subject under 70 characters. Must-nots: the reader's own past told back to him; a tool or a score as a sentence's subject; a verdict or slogan line; a benefit headline as the subject; subject restated as line one; three benefits with "so you can"; trial, billing or cancel terms; "Not this. That."; "Let that sink in."

Sequences. A serial from one named sender: each email is a single email (story or news, the turn, one destination) with a role a reader would recognize, and each non-final email ends on the next one's subject line or next scene. Welcome 5-7: set the stage (who is writing, what is coming, the first gift delivered, tomorrow's subject in the P.S.); the origin story opened at its point of highest drama, then the backstory and the wall; the epiphany that is the product's premise; the proof story (a documented customer when the ledger holds one, else the free taste: a working piece of the product given in the email, or the founder's own use from the story inventory); the objection a reader raised, answered; direct offer; last call with the real reason. Launch 10-12: three prelaunch pieces (the opportunity, the transformation, what owning it looks like), each teaching something real; open cart as the big reveal (the product as the answer to the prelaunch question, with the price and the deadline); four mid-cart (the biggest objection, the free taste, the questions readers asked, quoted from the ledger and answered, who this is not for); close day (morning: what closes and when; hours left: the one reason that matters; last call: a short courtesy reminder with the link); post-close. A launch needs a deadline with a mechanism in the offer block. Abandoned cart 3: within the hour, the cart held and one link, no discount; at 24 hours, the doubt that stops buyers answered, with the guarantee when the offer has one; at 48-72 hours, the last reminder, with an incentive only when the offer block holds one. Post-purchase 5: fast win; how to get the result; check-in; review request; next offer. Win-back 3: nine-word email with no links; what you missed; stay-or-go. Musts: every non-final email ends with a concrete reason to open the next; every email sells; the same offer and numbers in every email. Must-nots: an extended deadline; a guilt line; a list of questions the copy asks itself; a sold-count or momentum claim without a ledger count.

Webinar package. Every asset is written before the class and states only what the plan holds. Registration page: a short plain headline naming the reader's result (How to [result] without [what he hates], or news), the subject named and the how withheld, the date, time and time zone under it; a note from the speaker or host in his own voice (who, why now, one proof on file or what the class shows live, who it is for); three secrets as How-to bullets, each with one specific and its answer withheld; the button says the action. Confirmation page: the seat confirmed in one line, the email with the link, the date again, the true reason to come live, what to have ready. Invitation email (list traffic): the speaker's news and the specifics of what the class shows. Reminders: day before, morning of, 60 minutes, 10 minutes; each gives a reason to attend the earlier ones did not, the time with time zone and one link. Presentation, about 90 minutes, the price and link before the 60-minute mark: the speaker's name and his first-person promise of the specific result the viewer leaves with (this promise is the point, not a windup, and announcing the second agenda is not a pitch, Gate B); two agendas; proof shown as its documents, five testimonials in five minutes when the ledger holds them (none on file: those minutes go to the founder's own case from the story inventory, shown as its document, and the run each viewer makes in the trial, Section 4 item 7); one commitment typed in the chat; the biggest objections met early with proof; who it is for; the origin story told as scenes; case study from the ledger, or left out and its minutes given to the demonstration; three secrets, each stated as the result it brings, with its story, a demonstration on screen in secret 1 or 2, results (the ledger's; none on file, the result the secret produces, promised at full strength, rule 8), the related beliefs broken one by one with proof, trial closes the viewer answers, and the secret restated as what the viewer can now do; transition: the recap as what the viewer can now do, the yes questions, the cause (what one class cannot do for him), the permission ask, the name; stack: the core in three to five minutes, each part as what it lets him do and stop doing, with its strongest proof on file, bonuses second-best to best, restacked after each; price once, stepped down only from real prices on file, with its reframe; the guarantee in the speaker's words, matched to activation points; restack; urgency bonus with its mechanism; 30-minute countdown with the questions viewers ask, each answered with proof and closed with the link, the money and time closes said more than once in new words, the self-check questions and the hand-hold close. Replay page: comes down with the offer; the video starts where the class starts. Replay: five to seven emails over two to four days; the first says the replay is up and what it shows; one answers the common questions; the final day carries two or three emails of 100-150 words. Musts: offer, price, bonuses second-best to best, the offer block's scarcity throughout when it holds any, guarantee, objections cycled; 20-26 asks, each tied to what was just shown. Must-nots: teaching to completeness; apology transition; invented scarcity; an extended deadline; the reader's past narrated as fact in any asset's opening; a question or result attributed to an invented viewer; how the live class went, unless the user adds it after the class; a stack value or price rung the ledger does not hold.

Long VSL, warm or retargeted. One named speaker to one viewer, on camera or as words on screen in his voice, one spoken sentence per slide. Interrupt in the speaker's own present (what he holds, shows or announces); promise with mechanism; credibility as what he built, tested or called that the viewer can check; the bribe as the specific thing the viewer gets before the end; demonstration in the first third (a dated case from the ledger; with none, the product on a real example, the maker's own test, or the viewer's own run and how soon); catalyst; short discovery story from the story inventory, or left out; the plan with logic per step, each step with what it gets the viewer; objections as personas only when the ledger documents one; transition and stack with the button dropping at the price; price, guarantee, urgency (the offer's deadline, or the cost of waiting the mechanism holds), the last ask as the small step the guarantee makes it, CTA repeated. No minute allocations; test the first 500 words. Must-nots: biography first; the viewer's past narrated back to him; "our proven system"; the offer, price or button before the transition.

Short VSL, cold. One person on camera talking to one viewer. Interrupt in the speaker's present, tied to the promise (what he holds, shows or offers; a question the viewer answers about himself; the demonstration starting); promise, mechanism name and who is talking in one breath; catalyst; the usual fixes and what each costs, in the present tense; the demonstration on a real example with the usual fix failing beside it (or the viewer's own test); who it is for plus one disqualifier; next step with the one offer line (price or trial, and the guarantee when the offer leads with it); one real reason to act now. 2-7 minutes. Must-nots: stack; the viewer's past narrated back to him; fascinations where the product can be shown.

Meta and Instagram ads. An ad set is 3-6 variations, each a different argument for the same offer (the reader's situation now said as a fact, one checkable fact on file, the speaker's own use or story, the offer itself, a demonstration), each one person talking to one reader. The first 125 characters stand alone: the hook and what the reader gets, said the way the speaker would say it. Below the fold: why it works, one proof within two sentences, the offer in concrete nouns, one literal CTA. At least one variation runs a paragraph; one may run long when the offer needs explaining. Headline field: what the click gets, 27 characters (Instagram 40), sentence case. Description: the risk reversal or a count the ledger holds. Musts: "you" in the reader's present, or a call-out by trade or task. Must-nots: a personal attribute in Meta's list; the reader's own past or finances told back to him; a statistic about other people or a tool as the opener; "Are you tired of"; "Do you have [condition]"; "cure, heal, eliminate"; effortless-income claims; emojis; hashtags.

YouTube ad script. 0-5s the hook, said by the person on screen, with the product, the demonstration or the speaker in a real place in the first frame; 5-30s the usual fixes and what each costs, who is talking, and one disqualifier before the 30-second mark (a CPV view is billed at 30 seconds); then the mechanism shown working, proof (a ledger result, else the demonstration or the viewer's own test, with the guarantee beside the promise), offer, spoken and on-screen CTA in the same words, rehook with a proof the first half did not show, second CTA. Up to about 3 minutes. Must-nots: logo intro; "At [Brand], we"; the viewer's past narrated back to him; improbable-result claims (Google's sense: a dramatic change in health, money or circumstances promised as quick or certain, "lose 30 lbs in a week," "quit your job next month"; the result the product produces, said plainly, is not one).

TikTok and Reels. 15-45 seconds, under 90 spoken words. Line one spoken and captioned, with the product or the demonstration in frame (what the person on camera holds or is about to do, the result in the viewer's words, the offer, or a question the demonstration answers on screen); lines two to four show it working (or the speaker's documented discovery); one proof; CTA with location. Musts: proposition in the first 3 seconds; a real person on camera talking to the viewer; captions say exactly what is spoken. Must-nots: "Hey everyone!"; a contrarian maxim or a "POV:" line narrating the viewer; feature tour; emojis or hashtags in the caption.

Google responsive search ads. The searcher is scanning a list: each asset is a plain label of what he gets or does, in his words, the way a catalogue title says it. 15 headlines of 30 characters, most under 20, sentence case: 3-4 carrying the keyword whole, 3-4 benefit (the reader's result in his words, with a number only when the ledger holds one on the result, never a count of the product's parts), 2-3 proof (a ledger fact or count, the mechanism said as a fact, or the demonstration: "Test it on your own site"), 2 offer or risk reversal, 2 CTA. Every headline makes sense alone, beside any other and as a link; the first three read as one ad. 4 descriptions of 60-80 characters (90 at most), each one complete thought with its action. Must-nots: one headline shape; repetition; Title Case; slogans and quality words that name no thing; superlatives without a ledger row; questions; emoji or gimmick punctuation.

Advertorial and native landing page. A page the reader takes for an article. A person the ledger holds writes it as a reporter would write about a thing he found or a problem he solved. The label ("Ad," "Advertisement," "Paid Advertisement") above the headline, on the publisher page and on the page it opens. An editorial headline in the reader's own nouns, with no pitch: the plain what-everybody-ought-to-know, the odd true fact with its number, a quoted line, the scene, or a question about something he owns; a listicle's number matches its items. A byline and date naming the narrator (the speaker, the editor, a documented customer) and what he is to the product; with no narrator on file the speaker writes in the first person. The lead: 150-300 words a reporter would write with no pitch, opened in the middle of it on one fact with one move from Section 3 (a scene from the story inventory, a fact with a ledger ID that contradicts what he believes, the narrator's own event with a time and a place, or a test he runs now); the moment he lives with, said as the thing itself, so he feels it before he knows anything is for sale; the speaker by word 150; the band is a starting value, and the lead ends when he agrees. The usual fixes and their causes in his words, the cost of his current way said once, a comparison he can see when the ledger holds one, and one useful fact or check from the research he can use today: the copy pays before it asks. The mechanism as one chain, one person doing one thing or one comparison, in the narrator's voice, with the product's own terms explained or left out. Proof staged where the doubt arises, beside its claim: the narrator's own documented event, a ledger result with the typical result beside it, or the reader's own run told as what he will see, with the mechanism as the cause. The turn in the same voice: first the value already given, then the product in one plain sentence with its name before anything else about it, as the way to keep getting it, with the promise as a picture of his life with the result. Offer and links in the last third: what the click gets and what happens next in order, the price with a ledger ID, the guarantee with its reason, the reason to act now with its mechanism or nothing, the link at least twice in new words. 800-1,800 words cold; 400-700 retargeting; a native pre-sell that hands off to a sales page runs 200-1,000 words and sells the click. Listicle variant: each item a benefit with its specific and its proof, escalating problem, mechanism, proof, offer, first person when the narrator is a documented user. Words a ten-year-old knows, under the eighth grade (rule 40); feeling in every paragraph (rule 39); the label on both pages; the narrator exists; the headline aligned with the body. Never: "Promoted," "Sponsored" alone, or no label; the brand or the product named, used or pointed at before word 250; the brand name in the headline; "stuns," "reveals," "real reason," "last words," "years later," "you won't believe"; a synthetic before/after; a fabricated testimonial, review or narrator, or a "staff writer"; the opening in the shape of Section 7.1 tells 14 to 17; "Imagine" with no specific; a metric's name, a tool's term or the product's own labels; a lesson in how his trade works; the product's own work done by hand as the useful fact; a question the copy answers itself at the turn; "Click here to buy now!" as the turn; the seller's "we"; a founder, title or company line; a wait said to get him to act; "results may vary" or a reviews footer added by the engine.

Organic social. One named person posting to the people who follow him, about the thing that bothers them, in the words they use with a friend. Lead-in: line one is the moment he recognizes, said plainly with one opening move from Section 3, under 140 characters; line two pays it off with what it cost or what it felt like; line three is the promise as a picture of his life with the result, with its reason or a number beside it, and never the product. Main points: the turn (what the speaker saw or found), then the product in one plain sentence with its name before anything else about it, and that sentence survives every edit; how it works in two short sentences at most, as what it decides or makes for him; one thing he can check and one run with its numbers when the ledger holds them; the feeling of having it; one idea, each paragraph one new thing, every paragraph able to stand alone with no line pointing back to the lead-in. Words a ten-year-old knows (rule 40), feeling in every paragraph (rule 39), the low first price said once with its reason (rule 16), one CTA as verb, object, location and what the click gets him. Never: line one in the shape of Section 7.1 tells 14 to 18; a metric's name, a tool's term or the product's own labels; a lesson in how his trade works; the product named or pointed at in the lead-in; a founder, credential or company line; the reader sent to hire or pay someone for what the product does; how long anything takes; a list or count of the product's steps; a technical input explained; two sentences built on one frame; a pair of short sentences that land a moral; a second call to action; the hook said again at the end; "Thoughts?"; emojis; hashtags. The first comment carries the link and the one-line offer: that is the post's one statement of the full terms.

Product description. One person who knows the thing sells it to one buyer, the way the best catalogs do: in the first person when a speaker is on file, else in plain sentences with no pronoun. The name and the one result it is bought for, in the buyer's words; a third party's line about it only with its source named and its ledger row. The lead puts him in the moment he uses it, with one concrete thing in it (the saddle, the glare, the airport) and the feeling he already carries there, said as the thing itself (the cold hands, the gift that came late), and joins it to the one fact that makes this the answer: what it does for him there, the odd true detail that is the reason to believe, or the maker's own use on file; the product's first appearance is one plain sentence saying what it is and what it does for him (rule 38). Three to five facts, each with its number, material or named part and then what it does for him (2 mm steel, so it will not bend); among them one process specific or the maker's own test on file (with none, the mechanism as the cause), and the comparison with the dearer or usual thing when the ledger holds it; each makes him feel the use. One proof line beside the biggest claim: a named source or the maker's own test when on file; with none, the test he runs at home with the guarantee beside it, or the mechanism as the cause. The price once after the value, the guarantee as one plain promise with its reason, and what arrives, where the platform lets the copy carry them, and left out where the marketplace shows them elsewhere or forbids them. The specs in their own block, every fact he needs to decide, labeled, with units: name and number, materials, dimensions, weight, contents, care, compatibility and origin, as the ledger holds them. The review corpus supplies the substance, never the wording. Words a ten-year-old knows; the trade's everyday words are fine, and a part's catalog name is explained once or dropped (rule 40); a feeling in every part (rule 39). 60-150 words for a consumable; 150-400 for a considered purchase: the price sets the length. Never: "Elevate your [category] experience"; "crafted with care"; "designed to"; "premium"; a tagline after the name; Title Case; a superlative without the proof line; an adjective in place of a number; a feature name alone; "so you can" triads; a brand story with no row; a verbatim's survey wording; a review quoted, counted or composed without a row; a star rating or "loved by thousands" with no row; in the lead, the buyer's past told to him as a fact, a tool or a part as the actor, a verdict closing line one, or a slogan; the product pointed at before it is named; "At [Brand], we"; a lesson in how the thing is used; a price frame without the full price; promotional words a marketplace forbids; a deadline or a stock fact the offer block does not hold; a confession that reviews or results are not on file.

SMS. [Brand]: [the news or the thing held for the reader, said plainly to him, with the stock, scarcity or newness fact the offer block holds] [the offer line: its % and deadline when the offer block has them, else the price, the trial and the action] [link] [STOP wording]. With no deal, deadline or stock fact on file, one reason-why clause carries the message. 75-115 characters; never above 240. Musts: STOP wording; sent 9 a.m. to 8 p.m. recipient time. Must-nots: jokes; questions; product superlatives in a message with no deal; jargon; dollar-off; leaving out a deadline the offer block holds; a %, deadline, stock or scarcity fact the offer block does not hold; "waiting for you" when nothing is held; invented "almost gone."

Cold email. A short note from one named person to one reader. Why this reader: one fact about him the user supplied, with what in it made the sender write; with none supplied, who the sender is and why he writes to businesses like this one, said honestly, never an invented observation or a placeholder. What the sender does for businesses like his, said as what it gets him, with one carrier (a persona-matched case with its number when the ledger holds one; else the mechanism as cause, or what his current way leaves him with, in one plain sentence). One easy yes: interest in a specific thing made for him (a teardown, a benchmark, a run on his own numbers), or a question about his present situation answerable in a word. 30-100 words; subject 1-4 words about his own topic; no links in email one; follow-ups of one to three sentences in the thread, each bringing one new thing, the last a light closing note. Musts: tentative about the reader's situation, never about the result: what the product does for him is said in the indicative; 0-1 questions; what the sender knows beyond the one fact stays out. Must-nots: the reader's past or pain told back to him; an ROI percentage as the proof; a feature list; "I hope this email finds you well" on its own; "I'd love to schedule a call"; a day and time in email one; "Thoughts?"; "I never heard back" or any guilt line.

B2B pricing page. A buyer who knows the product and came for the price; the page keeps selling to the price. Headline: the result the plan buys, as a picture of his day with it or as what his old way costs set against the price, with the product's name and what it does for him said plainly in the headline or the deck; never "Pricing" or a line about picking a plan. Above the plans: the promise once more in new words with a fact the page has not used, then what happens after the click, in order (what he does first, what arrives, what he sees). Three plans make the choice which one, not whether: each opens with one line of who it is for and what he gets done with it (a crew of five quoting ten jobs a week); the price as a plain fact with its ledger ID; the differences as his situations (seats, sites, orders a month); the top plan as the anchor; the recommended one marked with a reason he can check; a plan's own name explained the first time it is used; each button saying what it starts in his words. The one term that answers the objection the research ranked first, said once with its reason (the trial, pay as you go, fair billing) and the worry it takes off him. Proof beside the plans only from the ledger: a documented customer at the plan boundary with his number, or a named review source with its count; with none, the trial told as what he sees on his own case, and the guarantee as a positive promise beside the price. The anchor: what the expensive way costs (the hire, the firm on retainer, the old tool, the weekends), a number only with its row, else said from the plain facts of his own work (the bookkeeper's monthly bill, the second van), with the gain said one step past the saving and the full price beside any per-day frame; a plan billed yearly and shown per month says so beside the price. FAQ headings in the buyer's own words from the research; each answer a plain statement in the indicative with its carrier (a ledger fact when on file, else the trial, the mechanism or the guarantee). CTA: the self-serve action in his words, saying what it starts, from the offer block; sales contact as a second path, never the only one. A feeling in every part, said as the thing itself (rule 39); words a cold reader knows, every term from his tool or the product's screens explained once or dropped (rule 40). 300-1,200 words. Never: "Pricing," "Plans," "Pick your plan" or "Simple, transparent pricing" as the headline; a slogan line; a per-seat fee as the headline; a persona label alone; four or more plans; gated pricing without a stated reason; a feature matrix as the only thing on the page; "Most popular" or "Best value" without a ledger count; "Contact us" as the only CTA; "Sign up," "Submit" or "Get started" with nothing named; the cancel-contracts-fees triad; "less than a cup of coffee"; a per-day frame without the full price; a price fight with a free alternative; a logo wall alone or "trusted by thousands" without a row; an invented objection; a hedge on what the product does; a lesson in how his trade works; a line selling what his current tool already gives him; a metric's name or a label from the product's screens left unexplained; how long anything takes unless the time is itself a reason to act; legal boilerplate; a confession that results are not on file.

Podcast and audio read. Produced spot: 55-80 words; the brand as the last word of the opening sentence, within five seconds, and twice more; one claim with its carrier; one CTA with the code. Host read: talking points, not a script; the host's own "why I use it" moment, asked for and never written for him (with none, he tries it on air); one mechanism; one proof (a ledger fact, else the trial, sample or guarantee as the reason to try); one objection; code and URL said twice, last, spelled when hard to hear.

Open, no spec yet: downsell, application and call-booking page, quiz funnel, cancellation flow, UGC video script, X thread.

---`;

export const VOICE_CAPTURE = `## 10. Voice capture

From three to ten samples of the client's own writing, selling copy preferred:

1. Measure sentence-length mean and deviation; share of sentences under six and over thirty words; fragment rate; Flesch-Kincaid grade; paragraph length; first-word class of each paragraph.
2. Extract greeting and sign-off; an idiom bank of 20-40 phrases; a dead-word list; humor type and rate; edge tolerance; formatting habits; pronoun ratio; stance markers; how they name the enemy.
3. Record three verbatim exemplar paragraphs: a lead, a proof passage, a close.
4. Build the story inventory: documented events, dates, numbers, named people and failures, each with a ledger row. This is the part of voice that cannot be imitated.
5. Set the readability band from the audience (consumer grade 5-8; B2B marketing, ops and executive 6-8; finance and legal up to 9-12; engineering plain syntax with precise nouns), then let the sample override it.
6. Write the profile as parameters, never adjectives.
7. Validate: generate 500 words in the profile, re-measure against step 1, and have the client mark every line they would not have written. Adjust the profile, never the draft.
8. Never penalize caps, fragments, exclamation marks or ellipses; human direct response uses all four at rates far above AI drafts.
9. A voice profile never brings back what Section 7.1 bans: em dashes, emojis, hashtags and the 7.1 tells and words stay out even when the samples use them.

---`;

export const EXEMPLARS = `## 11. Exemplars from the masters

Study what each one does; never reuse its words. Six words in a row from an exemplar, or from an example in Section 3, fail the mechanical check. Section 3 shows the openings; these show the jobs after them.

The writer also reads whole pieces before it writes one: original posts, emails and ads from other trades, one per master's move, each named for the move it makes (\`supabase/functions/_shared/engine/prompts/exemplars/\`, one file per format family). They are the register the piece is written in; every fact in them belongs to its own made-up business, and six words in a row from one of them fail the mechanical check like the quotations below.

- The speaker named, with the reason he is writing (Gary Halbert, the Caddy model letter): "My name is John Stebbins and I am a dentist here in Massillon. I've got a 2-year-old Caddy and it is in almost perfect shape and I have to get rid of it for reasons I'll tell you later."
- Story as proof, with a doubter to beat (Gary Halbert, the dollar-bill letter, 1990): "I was living in Ohio at the time and my friends laughed at my idea. They thought it was a big joke."
- The mechanism as one person doing one thing (Porter Stansberry, 2014): "Let's say you're a German and you want to buy oil from Saudi Arabia. You can't just pay for your oil in German marks (or the new euro currency), because the oil is priced in dollars. So you have to buy dollars first, then buy your oil."
- The reader's own test, told as what he will find (Pepsodent, the campaign Claude Hopkins began, 1927): "RUN your tongue across your teeth. You will feel film; a slippery sort of coating."
- Proof staged as an event, with its numbers (John Carlton, the Cage Fighter ad, 2000): "Tom put them all down. Fast. That 284-pound Texan, six foot eight, undefeated in 8 cage fights... begging for mercy after just 37 seconds."
- A bullet that promises a specific and keeps the how (Gary Bencivenga, the job-interviews ad, 1997): "The simple 12-word sentence that will make you the #1 candidate more often than you'd ever believe."
- The reason the deal is this good (Gary Halbert, the family-crest plaque letter, Boron Letters ch. 21): "The reason we have the So & So wall plaque in stock is because we needed to take some pictures of our products for publicity purposes and So & So is one of the family crests we used when making our samples."
- The speaker's own research as his stake (Gary Bencivenga, the Kurobuta ham letter, 2005): "Okay, call me a foodie fussbudget, but after months of research and tasting more than 100 hams, to my surprise, I couldn't find a single ham that satisfied me on every count."
- The speaker's trust beside the guarantee (John Carlton, the Free Gun letter): "I trust your judgment here. I wouldn't be writing to you otherwise."
- The close: the step, and the risk carried by the seller (John Carlton, the Cage Fighter ad): "Call. You risk nothing... because if you're not completely convinced, I will pay for your lesson."
- What arrives and when (Gary Bencivenga, the Kurobuta ham letter): "Your Kurobuta ham will be shipped for guaranteed delivery during the week of Monday, March 21st. This means that if you're planning to serve your ham on Easter Sunday, which falls on March 27th this year, your ham is guaranteed to arrive in plenty of time."
- A P.S. that adds a fact (Gary Halbert, the coat-of-arms letter): "P.S. If you are ordering only one report, send two dollars ($2.00). Additional reports ordered at the same time and sent to the same address are one dollar each."`;

export const OWNER_TELLS = `### 7.1 The owner's tells and words

These are the owner's rules. They apply to every piece in every format: the writer follows them, the tell edit removes what slips through, the detector counts them, and the copy chief fails a stack at Gate D. Each is a pattern, not a word list: rewording a banned pattern with a synonym or near-variant is the same tell.

| # | Tell | Example | Fix |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | "Quietly" for something that was not a secret | "Legiit quietly passed a million orders." | Cut it, or say who did not know |
| 2 | The three-beat that lands on a deep third | "Not a tool. Not a course. It's a system." | State the claim once: "It's a system." |
| 3 | A rhetorical question the copy then answers itself | "The result? More sales." / "Why does this matter? Because..." | Make the point: "You make more sales." |
| 4 | "Here's the kicker / the thing / the part nobody talks about," and any "Here's the ___" windup, reworded ones included: a label ("My rule:", "The fix:", "Bottom line:") or a count of what is coming ("Two questions decide it.", "I'd ask two things."), and in a short piece a count of steps, reasons or ways ("It takes seven steps.", "seven steps, in this order:", "Three reasons to switch:"), though a long format's announced structure (a webinar's three secrets, a listicle's numbered reasons, a VSL's plan) is not a windup; a colon reveal ("it leaves the decision with you: whether your site can beat them") or a "What ... is" opener ("What comes back is a decision:") | "Here's the part the score hides:" / "The score hides this:" / "My rule: build only the pages you can win." | Make the point directly |
| 5 | "Worth noting / remembering / keeping in mind" | "It's worth noting that the trial is free." | State the point: "The trial is free." |
| 6 | The buzzword pile | "double down," "at scale," "best in class," "move fast" | Keep one precise term, cut the rest |
| 7 | "Genuinely" and "meaningfully" as filler, and the same filler in other words ("truly," "really," "actually," "exactly," "literally") | "a genuinely useful report" / "exactly what your list needs" | Cut the word when the sentence means the same without it |
| 8 | The disguise frame: one thing presented as another thing in disguise | "X wearing a Y costume," "cosplaying as Y," "X dressed as Y," "X wrapped in Y," "X masquerading as Y," "X disguised as Y," "X in Y's clothing," "X with a Y veneer, skin or wrapper," "Sorting by difficulty feels like research. It's where bad content gets made." / "The keyword was never your competition. The pages ranking for it are." | State the claim directly: "X is really Y" or "X is just Y" |
| 9 | "The uncomfortable truth / the hard truth / the truth nobody wants to hear" | "The hard truth: most pages never rank." | Drop the signpost; a plain "honestly" once is fine |
| 10 | The pressure test | "stress test this," "sanity check," "pressure test that" | Say what is checked and how |
| 11 | Em dashes | "It works — fast." | A period, a comma or parentheses |
| 12 | Emojis | "✅ Instant access" | Remove them; never add them |
| 13 | Hashtags | "#SEO #marketing" | Remove them; never add them |
| 14 | The reader's own history or habit told to him as a fact in line one ("You / You've / Your X" with a past verb, a present habit he is accused of, or "If you" plus that habit) | "You paid a writer for a page your keyword tool scored as easy." / "You've paid for Semrush for years." / "If you pick keywords by volume, read this first." | Open on a fact instead (Section 3): a test he runs, a question he answers about his own work, a fact true of everyone in his trade, a loss told about someone else, or the speaker's own fact with a number. A plain record of his dealings with the seller ("You bought the Pro plan.") or a dated action on record is not this tell |
| 15 | A short verdict closing line one after the fact | "...scored as easy. It never brought in a single customer." / "..., and it never cracked page one." | End line one on the fact; line two carries the outcome with its size and source |
| 16 | A slogan line in the opening: "You" plus a benefit verb and a relative tail, with no number and no speaker | "You win rankings your site can hold, on keywords that bring paying customers." | Say the promise once, as a picture of the result with its carrier (the mechanism, a ledger number or the speaker's call) |
| 17 | A tool, score, list or report as the subject that says, rates, hides, hands or leaves the reader something, in the opening | "the difficulty score said it was easy" / "Semrush rated the keyword easy" / "the tool hands you a list" | Make a person the subject: a tool shows a number; a person reads it, picks, pays or decides |
| 18 | The product named or used by its own terms before the reader is told what it is | "you get a build, improve or skip call from Legiit Keywords" as the product's first appearance | Introduce it first in one plain sentence: what it is and what it does for him, in his words (rule 38) |
| 19 | The product's own work sent elsewhere: the reader told to do by hand, or to hire or pay someone for, what the product does | "six pages to pay a writer for" from a product that writes the pages / "check it by hand" | Say that the product does it, as what he gets (rule 38) |
| 20 | A namedrop: the speaker's title, company or other business said for its own sake, or an argument about what his business earns or loses | "I founded Legiit, and you can hire a freelancer there" / "my own marketplace loses that job" | Cut it; a credential is said only where the reader needs it to believe the next line, and never in a social post or a text ad (rule 8) |
| 21 | A wait the reader would sit through, said to get him to act | "your first plan shows in about 20 minutes" | Say what the click gets him; a time is said only when it is itself a reason to act ("in seconds," "today") (rule 18) |
| 22 | A metric's name, a tool's term or a product label a cold reader would ask about | "a difficulty number" / "search volume" / "a build, improve or skip call" | Say the thing in plain words once ("the number your keyword tool gives each search"), or cut it (rule 40) |
| 23 | A long word where the short one does the job, or a word from a report or a slide | "purchase" / "demonstrate" / "interpret" / "enquiries" / "a solution" / "performance" | The short word (buy, show, read, calls) or the concrete thing (rule 40) |

Tells 14 to 17 are read on each piece's opening only: its first five lines from the lead section on (never a headline above a lead). Tells 14 and 15 read line one, tell 17 lines one to three, tell 16 lines one to five. A quotation, a testimonial and a greeting are not lines.

Words to cut, or replace with the plainest word that keeps the meaning (cut it when the sentence works without it):

- AI words: delve, tapestry, realm, navigate, navigation, unlock, seamless, elevate, landscape, testament, bustling, harnessing, ever-evolving, ever-changing, multifaceted, meticulous, bespoke, tailored, captivating, enthralling, beacon, unleash, enrich, poised, towards, underpins, demystify, insurmountable, unprecedented, robust.
- Corporate filler: paradigm shift, cutting-edge, game-changing, transformative, groundbreaking, revolutionary, synergy, ecosystem, scalable, disruptive, move the needle, circle back, at the end of the day, when it comes to, the world of, not only (and its near-variant "not just X but Y"), next level, optimized, value-add, leverage.
- Guru-speak: abundance mindset, manifest, high vibration, the universe, alignment, holding space, sitting with it, authentic, authenticity, lean into, your truth, intentional, mindful.
- Empty hedges: just my two cents, for what it is worth, in my humble opinion, IMHO (and "in my view," "in my opinion," "if you ask me"), take this with a grain of salt, your mileage may vary. A hedge on the product's promise or its reason-why is the same tell: the conditional ("could," "might," "may," "can help": "it could help your pages rank" is "your pages rank"), a size nothing on file sets ("some searchers do," "in some cases"), and the speaker's call in the conditional ("I'd start with," "would have had you": "Start with the keywords you can win."). The fix says it in the indicative at full strength (rules 8, 10 and 20); a size a ledger row or a verbatim sets on a number ("up to 40," "most of the 40") is the claim's precision and stays (rule 11).
- Formal words: utilize (use), endeavor (try); formal connectives: furthermore, moreover, additionally, in addition.
- Legal boilerplate: non-waivable rights, legal rights apply, terms (and conditions) apply, not refundable, non-refundable, void where prohibited, subject to change, where permitted by law, all rights reserved, without notice, at our sole discretion, restrictions apply. Sales copy states the offer in plain words; the terms live on the page that takes payment. A disclosure block and the order form are exempt.

Exempt: words inside a quotation (a verbatim, a testimonial or a quoted source keeps its words), a proper noun, and a word that is the literal name of what the offer sells ("landscape lighting" for a landscaper, "wheel alignment" for a garage).

Structure: open on the point, with no windup ("In today's world," "Today I want to talk about"); end on the point, with no "In conclusion," no closer that restates what was already said, and no teaser, at the end or anywhere else, that withholds nothing specific ("earlier than you think," "that's where the trouble starts," "First, though," "I built a different way to pick them."); say each point once: in a short piece, a run of five words or more said again later is cut, or rewritten so the later sentence adds something the first did not, and so is a sentence that makes the same point again in new words; a promise and the line that proves it are two points, never a repeat, and a return to the promise in a new setting with a fact the piece has not used (the reader's own test, a documented case, the guarantee) is a new point, and the piece's first statement of the result it promises is never the line cut (a proof line that names the result again keeps its proof and drops the restated result); no confession or weakening admission (rule 12: saying a test, result, customer, review or site does not exist yet; adding that the product or its example is new, planned or unlaunched when the line is true without it; asking in the copy for proof to be added): cut it and keep the promise at full strength ("an espresso brand that plans to sell online" is "an espresso bean subscription"); a CTA, a price, a guarantee, a disclosure, a P.S. that restates the offer for the skimmer, an on-screen caption that shows the spoken line, and any repeat the format asks for (a restated guarantee, a repeated CTA, a code and URL said twice) are not repeats; no lead-in built from one-line paragraphs that each land a maxim, a slogan or a credential (the feed's AI cadence): each line says a concrete thing; a credential is never a sentence of its own ("I'm a licensed electrician."), it rides in a sentence that says what the speaker puts behind his call (the stake, the motive, the work he checks himself), in his own words and in a sentence shape this standard does not show; in a short piece the product's internals get one sentence, said as what the reader gets, however the lines are worded; no common word coined as a name for the product or its output ("the read," "the play") when the plain noun says it ("the report"); no line built to be quoted (a maxim, a slogan couplet such as "A keyword tool settles neither." answered by "Legiit Keywords settles both.", an aphorism such as "A keyword list is raw material.", a reframe written as an aphorism such as "every skip is a page you don't pay a writer for", or a "was never" reveal such as "A list of traffic numbers was never going to tell you which pages would.", a moral in parentheses such as "(nobody keeps paying for clicks that never buy)", or a promise tagline such as "You win rankings your site can hold, on keywords that bring paying customers.") when the plain sentence the speaker would say across a table makes the point; no formal connectives; vary sentence length (break up a run of medium sentences, let a few run longer); break walls of text into short paragraphs; plain words ("use," not "utilize"; "try," not "endeavor"); flatten formatting the text does not need (bold, headers or bullets) into plain prose.

The rule that matters most: one of these on its own is not the giveaway (real people write "honestly," and "worth noting"); the stack is, several in one piece. Get each piece as close to zero as the writing allows without mangling it. Natural and human beats sterile and rule-perfect: if removing a tell would make a sentence worse, rewrite the sentence so it reads well and still loses the tell.

The tell edit. Every draft and every revision passes this edit before the cold read; the revise step, passage rewrites and the factual review's rewritten lines follow the same rules. The edit removes the tells and words above and keeps everything else: the meaning and the argument (no opinion or strong claim softened); every fact, name, number, date, quote, link and ledger tag exactly as written (it never adds a detail a sentence lacks, and never changes one it has; a confession or weakening admission and a hedge on a promise are tells, not facts, and are cut); the speaker's voice and reading level (the same person on a good day, not another writer and not a corporate memo); the piece's language; the structure (sections, headings, lists and paragraph breaks, unless the formatting itself is the problem); and the length (close to the original word count). Copy that is already clean comes back close to unchanged; the edit never invents a problem to look useful. It reports what it changed in three to six plain lines, with no emojis or hashtags.

Scoring: tells 11-13 are Tier 0; every other 7.1 tell, word or windup is Tier 1 (weight 4) per hit (a repeated run of words counts from the second repeat in a piece, since one is common in human posts), and two or more in a piece are the stack the copy chief fails at Gate D and the mechanical pass sends back to revision.`;

